#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176678} :activity/id #uuid "6410482c-1a4b-55b8-afc6-f009af1b05ff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973 :topic/id #uuid "d050a7cc-fb5e-58e5-a1a7-41ca68d4da28" :topic/math-academy-id 3574} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175780 :question/id #uuid "e65b269e-a33e-5bc6-b13a-c3f12488f260" :question/math-academy-id "e-5527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394119}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "c6666ad3-c461-5111-a9f4-d5a6f4f8e51a" :step/math-academy-id 20163 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175781 :question/id #uuid "acaa8e8f-de36-56b0-8e02-ded7fd08118c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048861}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351198}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"} :step/id #uuid "3ddd6359-72c0-5eee-87d9-df029bef4a21" :step/math-academy-id 20164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175782 :question/id #uuid "da3e8948-1f17-5281-a2f7-2fb1732939d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5529"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048305}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375432}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8cbda370-20f2-5e6b-9780-b0122e4ee2a0" :step/math-academy-id 20166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176975 :tutorial/content "Let's find the eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-\\textrm{i}$ of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(1+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(-1+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = (1+\\textrm{i})x_1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $x_1\\in\\mathbb C$ to be the free variable, we find that the general solution to our system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (1+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that our eigenvector has complex-valued entries.\n\n**Note:** To check our result, we can show that $A\\mathbf v = \\lambda \\mathbf v.$ Computing $A\\mathbf v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand computing $\\lambda \\mathbf v$ gives\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo $A\\mathbf v = \\lambda \\mathbf v,$ as required." :tutorial/id #uuid "1bde3a85-d8df-5194-8581-77fe2108627c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4297 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8f4ddd6c-a17e-54f3-bade-a64685e04b36" :step/math-academy-id 26123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176644}}] :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176123}]}
